WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.868
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey,
it's Stephanie Wong,

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:03.900
and welcome to "Season
of Scale," a new series

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.450
I created to help you
build skill and resilience

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.070
into your design patterns.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.020
Today I'll be covering the
basics behind how to do that.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:11.893
Stay tuned.

00:00:11.893 --> 00:00:13.785
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:20.860
With everything going
on in the world,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.740
there's been undeniable
disruption for businesses.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.350
Social isolation around
the globe has settled in,

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.120
and traditional
brick-and-mortar companies

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.950
have been forced
to shift operations

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.340
into an online space, while
already online companies are

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.968
seeing a massive spike
in daily traffic.

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:37.510
This might have put
a lot of pressure

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.660
on you to adapt and evolve
your online architectures

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.460
and maybe face the
realization that you're

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.860
both resource- and
business-constrained.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.100
This rang true for Critter
Junction, a multiplayer gaming

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.140
company that's gained
massive popularity

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.370
in the last few months.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.200
Online players can
interact with one

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.120
another in a virtual
world that follows

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.770
life simulation as a critter.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.960
Building and operating a
gaming stack at this scale

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.330
requires a lot of
careful planning.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.670
So my team and I are
stepping in to help

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.130
Critter Junction revisit
their design practices

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.810
for ultimate global success.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.490
So far, Critter
Junction has been

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.310
great at running individual
machines on premise,

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.590
but hasn't really been
able to automatically

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.560
scale to many machines to handle
peaks and dips in traffic.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.070
At times, they end up running
over-utilized machines

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.500
and other times run
underutilized machines

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.260
and haven't built resilience
into their architecture.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.960
A failure in networking,
image updates, or peak load

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.080
could lead to a disruption in
their game players' experience.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.840
On top of that, Critter
Junction has its own business

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.470
and operational pressures.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.680
Their CTO is all
about becoming more

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.740
agile to adapt to
fluctuating user demands,

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.690
their developers are
focusing on reducing

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.270
the time it takes to
investigate failures,

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.550
and their operators
care about finding ways

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.690
to automatically
recover from failures.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.600
This is a lot to think about.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.940
But let's start by
first explaining what

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.400
scalability and resilience are.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.280
Scalability is the measure
of a system's ability

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.140
to handle varying
amounts of work

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:05.480
by adding or removing
resources from the system.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.960
For example, a scalable
web app can not only

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.140
work well with one user, but
also with 100 million users.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.300
And it can gracefully handle
peaks and dips in traffic.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.950
The good news is
the cloud gives you

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.210
the flexibility to adjust the
resources consumed by an app.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.890
The bad news--
without proper design,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.170
you could end up using more
resources than you need,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.140
like what Critter
Junction's done on premise.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.270
What you want, instead,
is to reduce costs

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.840
by removing underutilized
resources without compromising

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.630
performance or user experience.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:41.260
That being said, even scalable
apps can face disruptions.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.540
Without resilience built
in, system failures

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.790
can throw a wrench
in your operations.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.730
Resilience means designing
to withstand failures.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.440
A resilient app is
one that continues

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.620
to function despite failures
of system components.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.500
And this requires
planning at all levels

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.130
of your architecture.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.880
It influences how you lay out
your infrastructure and network

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.220
and how you design your
data storage and app.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.380
It even extends to
people and culture.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:07.078
Let's face it.

00:03:07.078 --> 00:03:08.620
Building and operating
resilient apps

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.380
is hard, especially for
distributed gaming stacks,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.140
which means multiple layers
of infrastructure and networks

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.130
and services.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:18.040
So over the season, we're
going to walk Critter Junction

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.800
through Google Cloud and
design best practices to help

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.350
them build both scalable
and resilient apps.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.690
All in all, these patterns
and best practices

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.120
will fall into three themes.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.940
Automation, because automating
your infrastructure,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.220
provisioning, testing,
and app deployments

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.610
increases consistency and speed
and minimizes human error.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.820
Loose coupling, because
treating your system

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.490
as a collection of loosely
coupled independent components

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.950
gives you flexibility
and resilience.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.520
And data-driven design, because
collecting metrics to your app

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.600
is critical.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.250
Decisions about when
to scale your app

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.830
or when a particular
service is unhealthy

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.210
need to be based on data.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:59.110
These themes are going to be
crucial to laying a stronger

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.600
foundation to scale
and withstand failures.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.360
Mistakes and outages
happen, but Critter Junction

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.070
is on a mission to
improve the design

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.355
of their app architecture
and dev processes.

00:04:09.355 --> 00:04:10.480
Stay tuned to find out how.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.370
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

